WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.950
As proven by its enduring popularity on Netflix,
The Office has taken its place in the canon

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:08.930
of television classics, shedding the notion
that it was somehow an inferior remake to

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:10.360
its British counterpart.

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:15.250
Instead, it revolutionized TV comedy in ways
that nobody expected — or even really noticed.

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:19.960
For the vast history of American television
comedy, the laughs have come via a tried and

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:23.400
true formula: exposition, then a joke set-up,
and then the joke.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.710
Cue the laugh track.

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:27.169
The Office turned that formula upside-down.

00:00:27.169 --> 00:00:32.000
Its comedy wasn't joke driven, but situational
and character-driven, coming from relatable,

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.170
realistic interactions.

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:37.680
And, as in real life, those give-and-takes
are sometimes extremely awkward and uncomfortable.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.080
"I'm supposed to be meeting someone named
Michael."

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.040
"Oh, that's not — yeah, I'm not…"

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:43.640
"Michael?"

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.600
"Michael."

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:50.240
It's a concept called "cringe comedy" because
audiences may literally squirm as the characters

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:54.820
on screen do awful but hilarious things that
would be unbearable in real life.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:58.230
When Michael Scott fails to deliver on his
years-long promise to pay for college for

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:00.290
a bunch of kids, it's extremely uncomfortable.

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:05.920
"Who here has done something stupid in their
lives, like skipped out on study hall or mixed

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:08.789
up the difference between A gym and B gym,
that sort of thing?

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:09.280
Show of hands?

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:10.240
Anybody?"

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:14.789
It's also very funny because it's so over-the-top
terrible — it doesn't feel like a contrived,

00:01:14.789 --> 00:01:18.659
silly sitcom premise because it seems like
just the kind of boneheaded mistake of which

00:01:18.659 --> 00:01:20.560
only Michael Scott is capable.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.609
Although there were certainly earlier examples,
like Curb Your Enthusiasm, The Office perfected

00:01:24.609 --> 00:01:27.440
and popularized cringe comedy for network
TV.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:32.040
For decades, nearly all sitcoms were produced
in the "three-camera" or "four-camera" format,

00:01:32.049 --> 00:01:35.840
in which many cameras would remain mostly
stationary and capture whatever happened on

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:37.799
a well-lit, fake-looking set.

00:01:37.799 --> 00:01:41.509
That style made episodes of everything from
Cheers to Everybody Loves Raymond feel like

00:01:41.509 --> 00:01:43.090
little stage plays.

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:47.030
While that method persists on shows like Mom
and The Conners, most sitcoms now go for a

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:51.209
realistic feel, eschewing the artificial and
saturated look of videotape for the warmer

00:01:51.209 --> 00:01:52.210
look of film.

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:56.320
They also shoot like a movie, with individual
shots of actors acting and reacting instead

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.459
of performing all at once on a soundstage.

00:01:58.459 --> 00:02:02.439
While a few examples of this "single-camera"
style existed before The Office, from Malcolm

00:02:02.439 --> 00:02:06.249
in the Middle to The Andy Griffith Show, the
format exploded after The Office presented

00:02:06.249 --> 00:02:09.149
itself as a shot-on-film fake documentary.

00:02:09.149 --> 00:02:13.709
A slew of sitcoms in the years since have
utilized a single-camera style since a "documentary

00:02:13.709 --> 00:02:18.460
crew" took us inside Dunder Mifflin Scranton
— including the likes of New Girl, Community,

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:22.280
Brooklyn Nine-Nine, and tons of others — and
it has become the dominant format in a way

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:23.760
it never was before.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.840
With the instant popularity of shows like
Big Brother and Survivor, reality TV took

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.930
off on American television around the turn
of the new millennium.

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:34.099
And then it stuck around, becoming a permanent
TV genre.

00:02:34.099 --> 00:02:38.769
That represented a major shift: American TV
had quickly evolved from fully-scripted fare

00:02:38.769 --> 00:02:44.260
to include programs ostensibly based on real
people in real situations, captured by ever-present

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:45.790
camera crews.

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:49.709
Something needed to come along to reflect
and comment on this rapid cultural development,

00:02:49.709 --> 00:02:51.510
and that something was The Office.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:53.980
It responded to reality TV by mocking it.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:59.650
"But seriously, you made a nine-year documentary,
and you couldn't once show me doing my origami."

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:03.950
The show's original premise became a bit obscured
over time, but the Scranton branch of Dunder

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:08.120
Mifflin was the subject of a TV documentary
called An American Workplace.

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:12.220
The Office was a "mockumentary" in the style
of films like This is Spinal Tap and Best

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:17.430
in Show, and that style pointed out that viewers
shouldn't take actual reality TV too seriously.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:21.250
With The Office laying the groundwork, that
fake documentary/mock reality show became

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:23.200
a prominent subgenre of comedy.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:27.760
The producers and network behind The Office
recognized early on that the way people consumed

00:03:27.769 --> 00:03:29.780
TV was changing fast.

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:33.739
It's fitting that The Office became such a
hit on a streaming platform like Netflix,

00:03:33.739 --> 00:03:37.400
because during its run, the show was one of
the first available via the internet.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:42.950
In 2005, Apple expanded its iTunes Store and
line of iPod music players to include video.

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:46.810
Users could pay a couple of bucks and download
an episode of a show, allowing them to watch

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:51.650
it on their computers when they wanted, or
on the go on a brand new video-enabled iPod.

00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:55.980
It sure beat taping episodes with a VCR, and
was even more technologically advanced than

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:57.170
a DVR.

00:03:57.170 --> 00:04:00.859
NBC was one of the first networks to sign
on for this way to make money off of the shows

00:04:00.859 --> 00:04:05.280
viewers could otherwise watch on TV for free,
highlighting The Office as a downloadable

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:06.200
option.

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:10.080
In addition to directly changing the way TV
is conceived, filmed, and distributed, The

00:04:10.081 --> 00:04:14.410
Office has another legacy: its cast and crew
scattered around Hollywood and became powerful

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:15.819
content creators.

00:04:15.819 --> 00:04:20.880
Steve Carell became an A-list actor, thanks
in part to a big 2005 — The Office debuted

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:24.650
just a couple of months before The 40-Year-Old
Virgin hit movie theaters.

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:27.940
Along with a bunch of big movies, he's stayed
busy behind the scenes on TV.

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:32.979
He even co-created the silly TBS comedy Angie
Tribeca, starring and produced by his former

00:04:32.979 --> 00:04:37.080
Office co-star Rashida Jones, who has also
worked behind the scenes on movies like Celeste

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.310
&amp; Jesse Forever and Toy Story 4.

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:43.040
John Krasinski became a filmmaker, directing
movies like A Quiet Place.

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:47.740
Mindy Kaling left The Office, where she both
portrayed Kelly Kapoor and served as a writer,

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:50.500
to create more TV, including The Mindy Project.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:55.020
And speaking of making TV happen, former Office
writer Michael Schur — a.k.a. Mose Schrute

00:04:55.020 --> 00:05:00.319
— co-created three beloved and critically-acclaimed
comedy shows: Parks and Recreation, Brooklyn

00:05:00.319 --> 00:05:01.850
Nine-Nine, and The Good Place.

